  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Lit Bento",
    "overview": "**Creative North Star: \"The Lit Bento\"**\n\nA familiar, soft-cornered bento grid on a near-black (Night) or porcelain (Day) ground, where the tiles are not interchangeable cards: they differ in size, material and job. The lead tile is alive, a canvas scene of Galeras over Pasto printed in ordered dither, with a car the visitor can drive. Everything else is still. The page earns its \"engineered\" claim through the artwork engine, not through terminal styling.\n\nArtwork is printed, not illustrated: every image surface (the scene, project art, the avatar) is rendered on canvas in the theme's own three-step cyan ink ramp, so one theme switch repaints all art at once. Photos resolve to true tone on hover, focus or tap. Dither lives inside tiles only; text and UI stay clean, high-legibility sans.\n\nDensity is relaxed: generous tile padding, one section rhythm, one accent. Type is a warm grotesque display (Bricolage) over a calm geometric text face (Onest).",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Two grounds (Night and Day), one cyan.",
      "28px tiles, 14px controls, pill navigation and chips.",
      "Canvas artwork in three dither inks per theme; pixelated rendering, 3 CSS px per cell.",
      "One living scene; hover develops photos; everything else holds still.",
      "Flat tiles separated by a 1px hairline, not shadows."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The One Cyan Rule",
        "body": "Cyan is the only hue. Any second colour on a surface must be a tint of cyan or a neutral; dither ramps are built from the cyan family per theme.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Ink Follows The Theme Rule",
        "body": "Art never hardcodes colour. Canvases read `--dither-ground`, `--dither-mid`, `--dither-hi` from their element, so the accent tile (`.on-accent`) and Day photos (`.photo-inks`) override inks locally rather than drawing in new colours.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Display Is Bricolage Rule",
        "body": "Every h1 to h3 and the wordmark use Bricolage Grotesque, bold and negatively tracked; body and controls use Onest. Headings balance their wrap.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Tabular Facts Rule",
        "body": "Dates and numeric facts use tabular numerals.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Hairline Not Shadow Rule",
        "body": "A tile is a tile because of its border and surface tone. If a new tile needs a shadow to read, the tone step is wrong.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "**Do** build every new surface as a 28px tile with a 1px line border on the tile tone, placed in the 12px/16px bento grid.",
      "**Do** render new artwork through the shared dither engine, reading inks from the three `--dither-*` properties so both themes repaint it.",
      "**Do** keep cyan as the only hue; use Deep Cyan for cyan text on Day.",
      "**Do** use the shared section frame (headline plus optional right-aligned intro) for every new section.",
      "**Do** mark unfilled content (quotes, CV, demos, photos) with dashed-border placeholders instead of invented content.",
      "**Do** keep motion to the scene, photo develop on hover, and 0.2s control feedback; respect reduced motion."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "**Don't** use dither as a full-page or behind-text background; it lives inside tiles as artwork.",
      "**Don't** use monospace or terminal styling for UI type.",
      "**Don't** add a second accent hue or a gradient.",
      "**Don't** add shadows to tiles; only the nav pill and the overlapping avatar float.",
      "**Don't** make a row of identical icon cards; tiles differ in size, material and job."
    ]
  }
}